<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<appendix xml:id="ck3-category-appendix">
  <title>Calculations with mutations and Serre functors</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>The projection and rotation functors are constructed in <xref ref="ck3-kuznetsov-category"/>. This appendix proves the projection and Serre identities used there, then computes the degree-three periodicity in the matrix-factorization description.</p>
  </introduction>
  <section xml:id="ck3-mutations">
    <title>The projection functor</title>
    <p>Let <m>E</m> be an exceptional object of <m>D^b(Y)</m> for a smooth projective complex variety <m>Y</m>. Identify <m>\langle E\rangle</m> with <m>D^b(\C)</m> by <m>V\mapsto V\otimes E</m>. Under this identification, the right adjoint of the inclusion <m>j:\langle E\rangle\hookrightarrow D^b(Y)</m> sends <m>F</m> to <m>\operatorname{RHom}(E,F)</m>. Evaluation is its adjunction counit. The triangle <md number="no">\operatorname{RHom}(E,F)\otimes E\longrightarrow F\longrightarrow L_E(F)</md> defines left mutation. Applying <m>\operatorname{RHom}(E,-)</m>, the first arrow is an isomorphism because <m>\operatorname{RHom}(E,E)=\C</m>; hence the last term vanishes. Thus <m>L_E(F)\in E^\perp</m>.</p>
    <p xml:id="ck3-cone-functor-anchor">For <m>A\in E^\perp</m>, apply <m>\operatorname{Hom}(-,A[k])</m> to the triangle. The groups from its first term vanish for all <m>k</m>, giving functorial isomorphisms <m>\operatorname{Hom}(L_EF,A[k])\simeq\operatorname{Hom}(F,A[k])</m>. This proves that mutation is the left adjoint projection to <m>E^\perp</m>. The functor can be constructed using the adjunction, or with the Fourier–Mukai kernel given by the cone of the evaluation map to the diagonal kernel; see <xref ref="bib-ck3-huybrechts-cubics" detail="Chapter 7, Section 1.3"/>.</p>
    <aside xml:id="margin-ck3-cone-functor"><p>The adjoint projection makes the evaluation-cone construction functorial.</p></aside>
    <p xml:id="ck3-mutation-projection">For the cubic collection, apply <m>L_{\cO_X(2)}</m>, then <m>L_{\cO_X(1)}</m>, then <m>L_{\cO_X}</m>. The second step preserves orthogonality to <m>\cO_X(2)</m>, since <m>\operatorname{Ext}^*(\cO_X(2),\cO_X(1))=0</m>. The last step preserves both previous vanishings for the same reason. The composite therefore lies in <m>\Ku(X)</m> and is <md number="no">\operatorname{pr}=L_{\cO_X}L_{\cO_X(1)}L_{\cO_X(2)}.</md> For <m>A\in\Ku(X)</m>, successive use of the preceding adjunction gives <m>\operatorname{Hom}(\operatorname{pr}F,A)\simeq\operatorname{Hom}(F,A)</m>. Thus <m>\operatorname{pr}=i^*</m> for the inclusion <m>i:\Ku(X)\hookrightarrow D^b(X)</m>. This proves the projection assertion in <xref ref="ck3-exceptional-collection"/>.</p>
    <p xml:id="ck3-projection-not-twist">The line construction there gives a useful check. The object <m>I_\ell(1)</m> has four global sections and is not in <m>\Ku(X)</m>. Mutation through <m>\cO_X</m> replaces it by <m>F_\ell[1]</m>. Applying all three mutations to <m>\cO_\ell(1)</m> gives <m>F_\ell[2]</m>. The ideal sheaf, its mutation, and its K-class have distinct roles.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="ck3-serre-rotation">
    <title>Restriction of Serre duality and rotation</title>
    <proposition xml:id="ck3-serre-restriction">
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>i:\mathcal A\hookrightarrow\mathcal D</m> be an admissible inclusion of Hom-finite triangulated categories, and suppose <m>\mathcal D</m> has a Serre functor. With <m>i^*</m> its left adjoint, the inverse Serre functor on <m>\mathcal A</m> is <md number="no">S_{\mathcal A}^{-1}\simeq i^*S_{\mathcal D}^{-1}i.</md></p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>For <m>A,B\in\mathcal A</m>, adjunction and Serre duality give <md number="no">\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal A}(i^*S_{\mathcal D}^{-1}iB,A)\simeq\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal D}(S_{\mathcal D}^{-1}iB,iA)\simeq\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal A}(A,B)^\vee.</md> These functorial isomorphisms characterize <m>S_{\mathcal A}^{-1}B</m>. The admissible projection gives the inverse Serre equivalence; equivalently the Serre functor itself is <m>i^!S_{\mathcal D}i</m>. This is the restriction theorem of <xref ref="bib-ck3-kuznetsov-cubics" detail="Section 2"/>, applied with the specified adjoints.</p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>For <m>F\in\Ku(X)</m>, the object <m>F(1)</m> is right orthogonal to <m>\cO_X(1)</m> and <m>\cO_X(2)</m>. Indeed, the derived Hom complexes from those line bundles to <m>F(1)</m> are those from <m>\cO_X</m> and <m>\cO_X(1)</m> to <m>F</m>. Left mutation through <m>\cO_X</m> preserves these vanishings because <m>\operatorname{RHom}(\cO_X(1),\cO_X)=\operatorname{RHom}(\cO_X(2),\cO_X)=0</m>. Therefore <m>R(F)=L_{\cO_X}(F(1))</m> belongs to <m>\Ku(X)</m>.</p>
    <p xml:id="ck3-rotation-formula">An exact autoequivalence takes the evaluation triangle for <m>E</m> to the evaluation triangle for its image. For tensor product <m>T=(-\otimes\cO_X(1))</m>, this gives <m>TL_E\simeq L_{E(1)}T</m>. Consequently <md number="no">R^3=L_{\cO_X}L_{\cO_X(1)}L_{\cO_X(2)}T^3=\operatorname{pr}(-\otimes\cO_X(3))</md> on <m>\Ku(X)</m>. Since <m>S_{D^b(X)}^{-1}=(-\otimes\cO_X(3))[-4]</m>, the proposition proves <md number="no">S_{\Ku(X)}^{-1}\simeq R^3[-4].</md> This supplies the adjunction and mutation identities used in <xref ref="ck3-serre-and-rotation"/>.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="ck3-matrix-periodicity">
    <title>The grading relation</title>
    <p>For the cubic equation <m>f\in\C[x_0,\ldots,x_5]</m>, write a graded matrix factorization as <md number="no">P_0\xrightarrow{a}P_1\xrightarrow{b}P_0(3).</md> Its homological shift is <md number="no">P_1\xrightarrow{-b}P_0(3)\xrightarrow{-a(3)}P_1(3).</md> Applying this rule twice gives <m>P_0(3)\xrightarrow{a(3)}P_1(3)\xrightarrow{b(3)}P_0(6)</m>. Thus the internal shift and homological shift satisfy <m>(3)\simeq[2]</m>.</p>
    <p xml:id="ck3-serre-final">We use the specialization of Orlov's equivalence stated in <xref ref="ck3-serre-and-rotation"/>: for the smooth cubic, internal shift corresponds to rotation on the residual category. Substitution in the preceding identity gives <m>S_{\Ku(X)}^{-1}\simeq[-2]</m> and <m>S_{\Ku(X)}\simeq[2]</m>. The equivalence and the correspondence of shifts are discussed in <xref ref="bib-ck3-orlov-singularity" detail="Section 3"/> and <xref ref="bib-ck3-huybrechts-cubics" detail="Chapter 7, Sections 1.6–1.7"/>.</p>
    <aside xml:id="margin-ck3-rotation-ordinary-twist">
      <p>Tensoring by <m>\cO_X(3)</m> on the entire derived category is an ordinary line-bundle twist. The cohomological shift occurs after applying the residual projection and the hypersurface equivalence.</p>
    </aside>
  </section>
</appendix>